Chapter: ProbeOverapplyTwoCallers

 COMPILER-18's precondition, isolated to ONE variable: a second CALL SITE.

 This and probe-overapply-one-caller.codex differ in exactly one thing --
 that file's third statement calls add3, this one's calls `sel` again.
 Same definitions, same statement count, same shapes. Measured 2026-08-25
 against seed 6CF4A8E0:

   one caller   control-flat: 6, first-call: 12, filler: 25, after: 15
   two callers  control-flat: 6, then a REGISTER DUMP at first-call

 `sel` is not recursive, has ONE return path, and its capture comes from
 its argument. None of that is the variable. What changes is that with two
 call sites `inline-single-caller` cannot fire, so the partial-application
 object is really built and really entered -- and entering it is what
 COMPILER-18 is about. Inlined, the closure is materialised at the call
 site and the trampoline is never reached.

 The zig plug's side of this was already written down: tier 14's prose
 says `control-pick` "survived there only because the pipeline inlines
 pick and materialises the closure at the call site; even-fn is mutually
 recursive and nothing can inline it". This is the same statement for
 bare metal, with the cheapest possible way to defeat the inliner.


  add3 : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  add3 (a) (b) (c) = a + b + c

  sel : Integer -> (Integer, Integer -> Integer)
  sel (n) = add3 n

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
   print-line-uni ("control-flat: " & show (add3 1 2 3))
   print-line-uni ("first-call: " & show (sel 7 2 3))
   print-line-uni ("second-call: " & show (sel 20 2 3))
   print-line-uni ("after: " & show (add3 4 5 6))
  end
